From a22d3e8def5f29378aa548b8c1c1754f0bd833b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peripheral Pretender setup <pp-setup@localhost>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:12:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 21/25] target/avr: AVRxt core (avrxt) for AVR Dx/Ex, tinyAVR
 0/1/2, megaAVR 0

The upstream AVR target models the classic cores only (avr5, avr51,
avr6): register file mapped at data 0, I/O at data 0x20. AVRxt parts
(AVR64EA48 and the other AVR8X families) differ in two ways that matter
before any peripheral works:

- Data space: the register file is not mapped; I/O starts at data 0, so
  IN/OUT/SBI/CBI/SBIC/SBIS add no offset and SP/SREG sit at 0x3d-0x3f.
  New feature AVR_FEATURE_IO_DATA (also right for AVRxm/XMEGA).
- Interrupts (CPUINT): taking an interrupt leaves SREG.I alone and marks
  level 0 or 1 as executing; RETI ends the level instead of setting I.
  Two levels, LVL1VEC, LVL0PRI with round robin, IVSEL vector base; the
  interrupt state lives in the CPU so the CPUINT device can mirror it.
  New feature AVR_FEATURE_CPUINT. NMI and the compact vector table are
  not modelled. SREG resets to 0 on these cores.

New CPU type "avrxt" (up to 64 KB of flash: no RAMPZ/ELPM). The firmware
loader accepts avr-gcc's avrxmega2..7 ELFs on it (gcc names these
architectures by memory size, not by core). Classic cores are unchanged:
an ATmega328P program with USART polling and a timer interrupt prints
the same on arduino-uno before and after.

Peripheral Pretender: AI-assisted, not for upstream QEMU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
